<page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Introduction to the Course</title>
      <p>
        Welcome to Calculus 1!
      </p>
      <p>
        What is the point of this course?
      </p>
      <p component="b" workspace="4in"/>
      <p component="a">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Calculus is the mathematics of change.
            </p>
          </li>
          
        </ul>
      </p>

    </paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
<example workspace = "2in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A red car passes through an intersection at noon traveling east at 40 mph. A blue care pass through the same intersection at 12:15pm heading north at a rate of 45 mph. If both cars keep going straight in the same direction, how fast is the distance between them changing at 1pm?
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>
<example workspace = "2in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      What should the radius and height of a cylindrical can be to minimize the surface area if the volume must be 1 L?
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>
</page>
<page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Introduction to the People</title>
      <p component="b" workspace="3in">
        Who am I?
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        I'm Dr. Matt Charnley. This is my first year at Wayne State, but not my first year teaching overall. I spent the last seven years teaching at Rutgers University in New Jersey, covering a wide variety of classes from Math for Liberal Arts to Differential Equations.
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        I am new here, so I don't know a lot about everything yet. If there's something you need help with, don't hesitate to ask me. If I don't know, I know who I can ask to learn these things about how Wayne State works.
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        Teaching is what I'm passionate about. It has been my thing for a long time and it's why I have this job right now. I want to see all of you learn and grow throughout this course so you can be successful in the future.
      </p>
      <p workspace="1in">
        Who are all of you? Take about 3 minutes to introduce yourself to the people around you. These are going to be your companions on this semester-long journey that we have together. When we come back together, I'll want everyone to introduce themselves with their name and intended major/career path.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
</page>
